      "body": "## Motivation\r\n\r\nOn MI350X/MI355X (gfx950), the Triton prefill attention kernel used to run faster for models with head size 64 or 128, such as gpt-oss, Llama and Qwen. #34461 made that change, with identical outputs. A later PR (#35770, for Kimi-K3) appears to have undone it by accident, so these models are back on the slower setting. The test #34461 updated still expects the faster setting and fails on MI350X; it runs only on MI300 in CI, so nobody noticed. This PR restores #34461's setting.\r\n\r\n@clintg6, was narrowing it intended for the MLA kernels? MLA head dims (576/512, 192/128) take their own branches and are not affected by this change.\r\n\r\n## Modifications\r\n\r\n- `extend_attention.py` (`_get_block_sizes_for_extend_attention`): restore #34461's condition and comment, `_is_gfx95 and Lq <= 256`. `BLOCK_N` is unchanged, so outputs stay bitwise identical.\r\n- No test change: the existing `test_extend_attention_block_sizes` already expects this.\r\n\r\n## Accuracy Tests\r\n\r\nThe reduction order (`BLOCK_N`) is unchanged, so outputs are bitwise identical; checked at kernel level on MI350X for head_dim 64 and 128 (8k-token chunks after 0-56k-token prefixes, and a mixed 4-sequence batch): output equal to the 64-row tile's with `torch.equal`.\r\n\r\n`python3 -m pytest test/registered/attention/test_triton_attention_kernels.py -k extend` on MI350X (`rocm/sgl-dev:v0.5.22-rocm724-mi35x-20261018`): before 3 failed / 5 passed, after 2 failed / 6 passed: `test_extend_attention_block_sizes` fails before and passes after. `test_extend_attention` and the head_dim-80 case of `test_extend_attention_unified_vs_regular` fail on MI350X with and without the change (tolerance checks, unrelated).\r\n\r\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\r\n\r\nMI350X, Qwen3-8B TP1, `python3 -m sglang.benchmark.serving --backend sglang --dataset-name random --random-range-ratio 1.0 --request-rate inf`, before and after alternating twice (pairs within 0.3%), output throughput:\r\n\r\n| Server | 8k in / 1k out (256 prompts, conc. 128) | 32k in / 256 out (64 prompts, conc. 16) |\r\n| --- | --- | --- |\r\n| `--attention-backend triton` | 1,468 -> 1,509 tok/s (+2.8%) | 101.7 -> 130.0 tok/s (+28%; TTFT 17.4 -> 12.6 s) |\r\n| deterministic mode, Triton prefill (the deterministic kernel takes its tile from the same table; measured with AITER decode, #44489) | 1,203 -> 1,376 (+14%) | 47.9 -> 69.2 (+44%) |\r\n\r\nNo change off gfx950, for head_dim > 256, or with other attention backends.\r\n\r\n## Checklist\r\n\r\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit).\r\n- [x] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests). (existing test)\r\n- [ ] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations).\r\n- [x] Provide accuracy and speed benchmark results according to [Test the accuracy](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#test-the-accuracy) and [Benchmark the speed](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#benchmark-the-speed).\r\n- [x] Follow the SGLang code style [guidance](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#code-style-guidance).",
